## State and failure contract

| State | Required behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| Valid reported value | Format the unit and preserve the integration's actual minimum, maximum and step |
| Editing a valid draft | Synchronise range, precise entry and step buttons without submitting |
| Empty, non-numeric, out-of-range or off-step text | Preserve the typed text; explain the constraint; leave the last valid thumb position unchanged; prevent submission |
| At an endpoint | Prevent another step beyond the bound; never wrap from maximum to minimum |
| Request pending | Prevent an accidental duplicate for the same target; retain the requested value and the earlier reported value |
| Request accepted | Say that the request was accepted or sent; wait for an appropriate reported observation before saying the device changed |
| Request failed or timed out | Retain the draft for review and the latest reported reading; offer a deliberate retry only when the target remains usable |
| Unknown or unavailable | Explain why the value cannot be adjusted; do not fabricate a numeric observation |
| Range, permissions or units change during editing | Revalidate the draft and explain the new constraint before any commit; do not silently clamp and send |
| Another person or automation changes the device | Reconcile the new observation with the local draft; do not overwrite an active edit on every unrelated update |

## Implementation contract

The documentation handler enhances `form[data-civica-slider]` only. `data-slider-min`, `data-slider-max`, `data-slider-step` and `data-slider-unit` describe the synthetic draft. They are not service-call parameters. Native range attributes must agree with these values. `data-slider-outcome` selects the local demonstration response, not a real backend result.

Use `data-slider-range` for the range and `data-slider-value` for precise entry. Buttons with `data-slider-step="-1"` or `"1"` move by one declared increment. `data-slider-output` shows the valid draft and uses `aria-live="off"` to avoid repeating every native range announcement. The range retains its meaningful `aria-valuetext`, including the unit. `data-slider-error` is associated with both inputs; request feedback uses the separate `data-demo-status` region. Do not announce every drag event through an assertive live region.

A real integration must use native Home Assistant capability and action contracts. Retain the existing commit policy where it is appropriate; adding a second `input` or `change` service listener can send duplicate requests. The shared examples do not replace `ha-control-slider`, colour-temperature controls, media seeking, climate cards or native More info dialogs. The theme and a documentation stylesheet cannot add exact-entry behaviour to every native control.

## Keyboard, touch and accessibility

Native ranges normally support arrows and endpoints; verify the actual target browser and Home Assistant control. Page Up and Page Down are not universal required shortcuts. Keep focus visible, preserve the label outside the moving thumb and allow all alternatives to work without a pointer. A keyboard alternative alone does not establish a single-pointer alternative to dragging. The visible step buttons provide that route for this component.

The [W3C slider pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/slider/) warns that touch assistive technologies need particular testing. Test VoiceOver and TalkBack on physical touch devices before making a production accessibility claim. The [WCAG dragging guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/dragging-movements.html) explains the separate requirement for a simple pointer alternative.

<h2 id="slider-acceptance">Acceptance and evidence</h2>

1. Adjust each specimen with arrows, endpoints, exact text and step buttons. Confirm all valid draft displays agree and reported text is unchanged.
2. Check empty text, a minus sign during entry, decimal steps, both endpoints, out-of-range values and off-step values. Invalid text must not be silently replaced or sent.
3. Confirm a local accepted response is distinguishable from a failure and neither implies a physical outcome. Changing the draft must not produce a network request.
4. Use keyboard-only operation, 320px layout, 200% text, forced colours and touch assistive technology. Check labels, units, focus, error associations and usable alternatives.
5. Turn off JavaScript. The documentation form must not submit or navigate on Enter; its limitations and static readings must remain understandable.
6. For native deployment, record frontend and integration versions, capabilities, commit policy, permission changes, remote updates, disconnection and request results. Reference examples are not evidence that these native cases have passed.

See the [native task test matrix](../home-assistant/native-task-accessibility.html). Automated documentation checks, manual screen-reader work and live-device verification are separate evidence classes.

